## The topological core

If a sequence has positive period $k$, then the subsequence at times $0,k,2k,\ldots$ is constant. If the whole sequence converges in a Hausdorff space, that subsequence must share the same unique limit. Thus the limit is the initial value.

<details><summary>Click to reveal the complete proof idea</summary>

Let $f(n+k)=f(n)$ for all $n$ and suppose $f(n)	o L$. By induction, $f(mk)=f(0)$ for every $m$. Since $mk	o\infty$, convergence of the original sequence implies $f(mk)	o L$. The constant subsequence also converges to $f(0)$. Uniqueness of limits yields $L=f(0)$. Applying the same argument to every residue class modulo $k$ shows that a convergent periodic sequence is constant.

</details>

