## Instantaneous motion versus temporal averaging

The localized shift remains a point mass forever, so its instantaneous total-variation distance from uniform is $1-1/N$. Yet averaging observations over time counts visits, and complete laps distribute those counts equally.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the averaging bound</summary>

Write $T=qN+r$ with $0\le r<N$. During the first $T$ steps, each vertex is visited either $q$ or $q+1$ times. Hence every averaged coordinate differs from $1/N$ by at most $1/T$, and the total-variation distance is at most $N/(2T)$. If $N$ divides $T$, the average is exactly uniform.

</details>

